   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "&quot;Is there a way to just write the vector&#39;s values to the text file immediately without line breaks?&quot; - You need with or without line breaks??"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 6406393,
   "parent_id": 6406356,
   "author_user_id": 22517,
   "author_display_name": "Peter",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/6406393",
   "score": 12,
   "created_utc": "2011-06-20T02:45:03Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2011-06-20T02:45:03Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>Maybe I have missed something, but what is wrong with:</p>\n\n<pre><code>std::ofstream f(\"somefile.txt\");\nfor(vector&lt;X&gt;::const_iterator i = v.begin(); i != v.end(); ++i) {\n    f &lt;&lt; *i &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>That avoids having to do potentially quadratic string concatenation, which I assume is what's killing your run-time.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 6406411,
   "parent_id": 6406356,
   "author_user_id": 78845,
   "author_display_name": "johnsyweb",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/6406411",
   "score": 85,
   "created_utc": "2011-06-20T02:51:07Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2023-01-02T05:13:19Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2023-01-02T05:13:19Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
   "body_html": "<p>Using <a href=\"http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/io/basic_ofstream\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><code>std::ofstream</code></a>, <a href=\"http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/iterator/ostream_iterator\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><code>std::ostream_iterator</code></a> and <a href=\"http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/algorithm/copy\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><code>std::copy()</code></a> is the usual way to do this. Here is an example with <a href=\"http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/string/basic_string\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><code>std::string</code></a>s using C++98 syntax (the question was asked pre-C++11):</p>\n<pre><code>#include &lt;fstream&gt;\n#include &lt;iterator&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main()\n{\n    std::vector&lt;std::string&gt; example;\n    example.push_back(&quot;this&quot;);\n    example.push_back(&quot;is&quot;);\n    example.push_back(&quot;a&quot;);\n    example.push_back(&quot;test&quot;);\n\n    std::ofstream output_file(&quot;./example.txt&quot;);\n    std::ostream_iterator&lt;std::string&gt; output_iterator(output_file, &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    std::copy(example.begin(), example.end(), output_iterator);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>[Some years later]</p>\n<p>A more modern implementation may look like:</p>\n<pre><code>#include &lt;fstream&gt;\n#include &lt;iterator&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main()\n{\n    std::vector&lt;std::string&gt; example { &quot;This&quot;, &quot;is&quot;, &quot;a&quot;, &quot;test&quot; };\n\n    std::ofstream output_file(&quot;./example.txt&quot;);\n\n    std::ostream_iterator&lt;std::string&gt; output_iterator(output_file, &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    std::copy(std::begin(example), std::end(example), output_iterator);\n}\n</code></pre>\n"
  },
  {
